From u448410b8993f8a6a at groups.google.com  Tue Jun  3 04:01:54 2014
From: u448410b8993f8a6a at groups.google.com (kmw...@ucdavis.edu)
Date: Tue, 03 Jun 2014 04:01:54 +0000
Subject: Not a question, but a resource
Message-ID: <7_GGt7LURaM.1NyjhgQeXLIJ@davis-rug.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/davis-rug/c/7_GGt7LURaM/m/1NyjhgQeXLIJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Today we discussed non-parametric analyses for count and proportion data, and I wanted to add this resource ("A Robust Procedure for Comparing Multiple Means under Heteroscedasticity in Unbalanced Designs") to the discussion. It appears that the packages multcomp and sandwich (as someone else also mentioned today, thanks) provide options for conducting non-parametric analyses where variances are heteroskedastic, and also allows for all pairwise comparisons or just comparisons to a control (a la Dunnett). Caveat: I have not tried this with my dataset, and not sure how it deals with overdispersion, but this might help with yours!

From u448410b8993f8a6a at groups.google.com  Tue Jun  3 21:55:59 2014
From: u448410b8993f8a6a at groups.google.com (kmw...@ucdavis.edu)
Date: Tue, 03 Jun 2014 21:55:59 +0000
Subject: Question re: zero inflated negative binomial model for count data
Message-ID: <2lVsKeaWJLQ.8B6a4vfJdhsJ@davis-rug.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/davis-rug/c/2lVsKeaWJLQ/m/8B6a4vfJdhsJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi all, I am attempting to run zero-inflated negative binomial model for count data within 6 treatment groups, but one of the groups has absolutely no variance (all data points are zero), and when I try to run the model I get the following error:

Call: zeroinfl(formula = Max.Sept ~ Treatment, data = np_d,
         dist = "negbin", EM = TRUE)

Error in solve.default(as.matrix(fit$hessian)) :
  Lapack routine dgesv: system is exactly singular: U[10,10] = 0

I have searched the help forums, but not found any clear answers other than to simplify my model (I only have one independent variable, can't simplify it much more).
I am thinking that perhaps the lack of variance in one of the treatment groups causes the test to fail because it cannot estimate the parameter, but that is only my guess. I could artificially add some variance (like inject some 1's into the misbehaving treatment group, but then I reduce the number of zeros, and this seems a bit hokey anyway).

Does anyone have any suggestions? Thank you!!

From u8954cb0a46bd963a at groups.google.com  Tue Jun 10 22:36:24 2014
From: u8954cb0a46bd963a at groups.google.com (Bonnie Dixon)
Date: Tue, 10 Jun 2014 22:36:24 +0000
Subject: using apply() to draw multiple lines with abline()
Message-ID: <abeN9HmutGY.xBGJ4Amj70EJ@davis-rug.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/davis-rug/c/abeN9HmutGY/m/xBGJ4Amj70EJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Dear D-RUG group mind;

Does anyone know why using an apply() statement to draw multiple lines on a plot with abline() returns "NULL" in addition to drawing the lines?  I am creating a plot in an R Markdown PDF document, and my document would look nicer if it didn't have "NULL" printed along with the plot.

Here is an example of the problem:

> plot(1)
> x <- cbind(c(0.8, 1.0, 1.2), c(0, 0, 0))
> apply(x, 1, abline)
NULL

Bonnie

From uea15a06de541fa42 at groups.google.com  Tue Jun 10 22:45:33 2014
From: uea15a06de541fa42 at groups.google.com (Vince S. Buffalo)
Date: Tue, 10 Jun 2014 22:45:33 +0000
Subject: Re: [D-RUG]: using apply() to draw multiple lines with abline()
Message-ID: <abeN9HmutGY.ltpoIIFD2ngJ@davis-rug.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <abeN9HmutGY.xBGJ4Amj70EJ@davis-rug.groups.google.com>
References: <abeN9HmutGY.xBGJ4Amj70EJ@davis-rug.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/davis-rug/c/abeN9HmutGY/m/ltpoIIFD2ngJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi Bonnie,

This is because the function abline isn't returning anything each iteration in apply(). apply() needs to return something so it returns NULL. This isn't necessarily a problem; the output can be ignored. If it bothers you, you can use a for loop or capture the output to a variable in local scope:

# maybe something like:
> local(a <-apply(x, 1, abline))
> a # doesn't pollute your environment
Error: object 'a' not found

HTH,
Vince

